{{!-- A2's side rail: the contents list, then the share actions.

STICKY ONLY WHILE THERE IS A SECOND COLUMN. Below `stack` the grid collapses to one column,
and a sticky child of a one-column grid pins itself while the rest of the column scrolls
past underneath — the article would run straight through the contents list and the share
buttons. `max-stack:static` is what stops that, and it is the same breakpoint token the
grid in post/a2.hbs collapses at, so the two cannot disagree.

`top-[120px]` is the design's own offset. It clears the bar with room to spare rather than
tracking its height: the reading bar is 76px, and a rail that starts exactly at the bar's
edge reads as a collision.

The contents list goes when the article goes. A gated post has no sections to jump to — its
free portion is the opening, which is where the headings are not — and a list of anchors into
a body the reader cannot read is navigation that leads nowhere. The share block stays: the
post is still shareable, and the design keeps it. --}}
<aside
	aria-label="{{t "Post details"}}"
	class="max-stack:static sticky top-[120px]">
	{{#if access}}
		{{> "post/toc" variant="rail"}}
	{{/if}}

	{{> "post/share" rail=true}}
</aside>
